# Build brief: portfolio scenario levers (scope, date, dependency, focus)

Written for a Claude Code session on `nicrpayne/kit-gap-audit`. Third
brief in this series — read `CAPACITY_POOL_AND_PORTFOLIO_BUILD_BRIEF.md`
and `DESIGN_LANGUAGE_AND_MOMENTUM_BUILD_BRIEF.md` first, since this one
assumes both exist and builds on the preview/override mechanism from the
first and, ideally, the visual language from the second.

## The motivating gap

`/portfolio` currently lets Nic pull one lever live on a call: who's
allocated where. That answers "what if we hired/moved people." It
doesn't answer the other questions that actually come up in the same
conversations: "what if we cut this feature," "what if we just accepted
a later date," "what if Platform wasn't blocking us," or "what if people
weren't split three ways." Those are all real, common asks, and none of
them are currently possible without leaving the app.

## When to build this

**Not yet.** Do not start this until the design/momentum brief has
landed — at minimum on `/forecast`, ideally further along — and has been
checked in with Nic. Same reasoning as before: two large efforts on one
branch at once is how things get hard to review, and these levers should
visually adopt whatever chip/pill language the design brief establishes
(the "Try" chip pattern it uses for existing scenario presets) rather
than inventing a second visual style that then has to be reconciled
later. When Nic says go, build this as its own branch off the
then-current base.

## Levers to build, recommended order (cheapest/lowest-risk first)

### 1. Target-date lever, both directions

Two views of the same already-computed data — neither needs a new
simulation run, just a different read of the sorted trial-days array
`computeForecast`/`runPortfolioSimulation` already produces:

- **Given a hypothetical target date, what's the confidence?** Re-run
  the existing confidence-at-target calculation against a different
  date. Useful for "what if we just accepted [date]."
- **Given a target confidence, what date does that require?** The
  inverse — look up the Nth percentile day-count in the same sorted
  array and convert to a calendar date. Useful for "what can I safely
  promise if I want to be 85% sure, not 50%."

Both are pure reads against results that already exist post the Phase
1.5 lockstep fix — for a scope with dependencies, its own trial-days
array already has the correlated dependency effect baked in, so this
lever doesn't need to touch `portfolio.ts` or `simulate.ts` at all,
dependent or not. Confirm that's still true before writing code; don't
assume without checking the current shape of what those functions
return.

### 2. Context-switch / focus toggle

Before building anything new here, **check what already exists** —
`PortfolioSettings.contextSwitchCost` and `resolve.ts`'s capacity-penalty
math for split allocations were both built in Phase 1, and a
"Context-switch cost" slider was already visible in the Allocations
panel during testing. This lever may be mostly UI framing, not new
plumbing: a clear before/after comparison ("this person split three ways
vs. focused on one") rather than new calculation. Confirm the current
behavior against real data first, then figure out what's actually
missing — likely just making the effect legible (a delta shown when the
slider moves), not the underlying math.

### 3. Dependency-relief preview

"What if Platform's blocking piece went away" — a hypothetical,
preview-only removal (or resolution) of one scope's entry in another's
`dependsOnScopeIds`, run through the exact same `runPortfolioSimulation`
lockstep orchestration used for the real preview endpoint, just with a
modified dependency edge for that one call. Nothing persisted. This
should be a thin extension of the existing `/api/portfolio/preview`
override shape (add an optional dependency-override alongside the
existing allocation overrides), not new orchestration — the lockstep
math, cycle detection, and topological ordering are already built and
proven; reuse them exactly as they are.

### 4. Scope-cut lever (ticket include/exclude)

The most involved of the four. Needs:

- A UI surface listing the tickets/items feeding a given scope's
  forecast (likely per-scope on `/forecast`, or inline on `/portfolio`
  — your call on placement, but flag which you chose and why), with a
  toggle per ticket and visible context on whether it has a real
  estimate or a placeholder, since that's relevant to whether excluding
  it is a real scope decision or just removing noise.
- Extending the override system so a preview can carry an optional
  excluded-ticket-ids set per scope alongside allocation and dependency
  overrides, and confirming exactly where in `buildForecastInputs`/
  `buildScopeSimInputs` the item list gets assembled so exclusion can be
  applied there without touching the Monte Carlo sampling itself.
- Same preview-not-persisted default as everything else, with an
  explicit save step if Nic wants to actually commit to descoping
  something (this one has real product implications if saved, more than
  a hypothetical allocation does, so don't make save the default path
  or make it too easy to fat-finger).

## Where these live in the UI

Follow the existing "Try" chip pattern (changes something, shows a
result) established by the allocation presets and formalized by the
design brief — these are all "Try" levers, not "Ask" levers. Don't
invent a fifth interaction pattern for four features that are all the
same shape: pick a hypothetical, see the portfolio recompute, decide
whether to save.

## Non-goals

- Don't touch `lib/forecast/simulate.ts`'s core Monte Carlo math for any
  of these — all four are either pure reads of existing results, or
  reuses of the existing lockstep orchestration with different inputs.
- Don't build ticket-level editing (renaming, re-estimating, reassigning
  a ticket to a different scope) — the scope-cut lever is include/exclude
  only, not a ticket editor.
- Don't build automatic "here's what I'd recommend cutting" suggestions
  — that's Ask-chip/explain-endpoint territory from the design brief,
  a separate capability, not this brief.
- Don't make any of these four persist by default — preview-first,
  explicit save, same pattern as the allocation lever from Phase 2.

## Acceptance test

On a call, Nic should be able to: exclude two placeholder-estimate
tickets from JSA and watch the date pull in; push iTrack's target date
out a month and watch confidence jump with no capacity change; preview
Platform's blocking piece going away and watch JSA and iTrack realign
together (same lockstep correlation proof style as Phase 1.5 — prove
this one explicitly, don't assume reuse guarantees it); and flip a
split-allocated person to single-project focus and watch the
context-switch penalty visibly disappear from that scope's forecast.
None of the four should require a page reload or leaving `/portfolio` or
`/forecast`.
